To prevent any potential out-of-bounds write (buffer overflow) in `trace_frame`, we should defensively check if `trace->size` is already at or exceeds `BACKTRACE_SIZE` before writing to `trace->pc`. Although `backtrace_simple` is expected to stop when the callback returns non-zero, adding this guard is a robust security practice.

```
+	if (trace->size >= BACKTRACE_SIZE)
+		return 1;
+	if (pc == 0 || pc == UINTPTR_MAX)
+		return 1;
+	trace->pc[trace->size++] = pc;
```

[🤖] **[minor · performance] Zeroing recycled pages via safemem.Zero slows Allocate up to 14%**

Recycled waste pages used to be zeroed with `clear()`. That calls the Go runtime's memclr, which uses `REP STOSQ` for 2 KiB–32 MiB on CPUs with ERMS. They are now zeroed with `safemem.Zero`, and safecopy's `memclr` is a 16-byte SSE loop. `Allocate()` on recycled pages serves private-anonymous page faults, COW breaks and tmpfs writes. It is now measurably slower for cache-cold 64 KiB–2 MiB ranges and for hot 4 KiB pages. `pkg/sentry/pgalloc` is performance-sensitive, and the PR includes no benchmark data.

**Suggested fix:** Add benchstat data for the recycled path to the PR, and reduce the cost in one of two ways:

- (a) Give safecopy's amd64 `memclr` a `REP STOSQ` path for n ≥ 2 KiB on ERMS CPUs, as the runtime does. The safecopy signal handler already covers any PC inside `memclr`, and this would also speed up other `safemem.Zero` users.
- (b) Keep `clear()` for recycled blocks that `MADV_POPULATE_WRITE` actually populated, and use `safemem.Zero` only for pages that population skipped. `MADV_POPULATE_WRITE` reports a backing failure as EFAULT instead of raising SIGBUS.

I did not verify either option.

<details><summary>Details (evidence, reproduction and test code)</summary>

**Evidence:** I added `BenchmarkAllocateRecycled` to `pkg/sentry/pgalloc/pgalloc_test.go`. It recycles committed waste pages with `AllocateCallerIndirectCommit`; the "cold" cases rotate over 256 MiB. Setup: the same test file at the merge-base and at the PR, both built with `bazel build -c opt //pkg/sentry/pgalloc:pgalloc_test`. 16 alternating runs per side (`-test.run='^$' -test.bench=BenchmarkAllocateRecycled`), pinned with `taskset` to one CPU. Machine: Intel Xeon 2.8 GHz with ERMS/AVX2, Go 1.27.2. p-values are from a Mann-Whitney U test.

| case | base | head | delta | p |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 4K hot | 319 ns | 344 ns | +7.9% | 1e-6 |
| 64K hot | 2.62 µs | 2.70 µs | +3.2% | 3e-4 |
| 2M hot | 105.9 µs | 107.9 µs | +1.9% | 0.002 |
| 4K cold | 1048 ns | 994 ns | −5.2% | 2e-5 |
| 64K cold | 8.44 µs | 9.43 µs | +11.8% | 2e-5 |
| 2M cold | 235.7 µs | 269.4 µs | +14.3% | 1e-6 |

An unpinned run gave +17% (64K cold) and +18% (2M cold). Allocations are unchanged (0 allocs/op on both sides).

`BenchmarkZeroPrimitive` isolates the cause on the same cold memfd memory. `safemem.Zero` compared with `clear()` is −1% at 4K, +19% at 64K and +21% at 2M. The runtime's `memclr_amd64.s` switches to `REP STOSQ` (no read-for-ownership) at 2 KiB when ERMS is present. `pkg/safecopy/memclr_amd64.s` has only the MOVOU loop.

**Reproduced with a test** (`pkg/sentry/pgalloc/pgalloc_test.go`): `bazel build -c opt //pkg/sentry/pgalloc:pgalloc_test && mkdir -p /tmp/pr15181 && cp -f bazel-bin/pkg/sentry/pgalloc/pgalloc_test_/pgalloc_test /tmp/pr15181/head_test ; bazel build -c opt //pkg/sentry/pgalloc:pgalloc_test && cp -f bazel-bin/pkg/sentry/pgalloc/pgalloc_test_/pgalloc_test /tmp/pr15181/base_test ; 'cd /tmp/pr15181 && for i in $(seq 1 16); do taskset -c 29 ./base_test -test.run="^$" -test.bench=BenchmarkAllocateRecycled -test.benchtime=1s | grep ^Benchmark >> base_pin.txt; taskset -c 29 ./head_test -test.run="^$" -test.bench=BenchmarkAllocateRecycled -test.benchtime=1s | grep ^Benchmark >> head_pin.txt; done; python3 stats.py base_pin.txt head_pin.txt' (stats.py in /tmp/pr15181 computes median, CV and a Mann-Whitney U p-value). Primitive check: taskset -c 29 ./head_test2 -test.run='^$' -test.bench=BenchmarkZeroPrimitive` → With n=16 per side, recycled Allocate() is 11.8% slower at 64K cold, 14.3% slower at 2M cold and 7.9% slower at 4K hot than at the merge-base (all p<0.01). The primitive benchmark shows safemem.Zero is about 20% slower than clear() for cold zeroing of 64K and larger.

**Test code:**

```diff
